# Consistency first

- Match existing patterns in this repo. Do not invent a second way to do the same thing.
- If a helper, naming style, or structure already exists for something, reuse it everywhere that logic appears.
- When the user points at reference code, treat that as the source of truth and update all related call sites in one pass.
- Before finishing, check related files for the old/inconsistent pattern and align them.

# When consistency is unclear — ask

If there is **no clear standard** in the codebase, or it is **hard to stay consistent** (multiple existing styles, ambiguous naming, unclear where logic should live), **ask the user** before changing code:

> I don't see a clear pattern for this. Is there a reference or approach you want me to follow?

Do not guess and create a new parallel pattern. Only ask when the standard is genuinely unclear — not before every routine change that already has an obvious match in the repo.
